    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-glycemic index (GI) foods are quickly digested and absorbed, causing a rapid increase in blood glucose levels. This triggers an insulin response, which can lead to insulin resistance and blood sugar spikes. In contrast, low-GI foods are digested and absorbed more slowly, resulting in a gradual increase in blood glucose and a smaller, more sustained insulin response. This can help regulate blood sugar levels and improve insulin sensitivity. By choosing low-GI foods, individuals can reduce their risk of developing chronic diseases like type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome.",
